99
00:07:37,266 --> 00:07:41,554
In this lesson, a BC topic, we'll model
growth that levels off.

100
00:07:42,699 --> 00:07:49,003
Real populations run out of room or food.
Growth slows as the population nears a

101
00:07:49,003 --> 00:07:51,446
limit, the carrying capacity L.

102
00:07:51,896 --> 00:07:54,093
Exponential growth can't capture that.

103
00:07:55,233 --> 00:08:01,590
The logistic equation is d P d t equals k
P times one minus P over L.

104
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:07,117
When P is small, it's nearly exponential.
When P nears L, growth stops.

105
00:08:08,266 --> 00:08:14,725
Using partial fractions and separation,
the solution is P equals L over one plus A

106
00:08:14,725 --> 00:08:16,458
e to the negative k t.

107
00:08:16,908 --> 00:08:19,596
As t goes to infinity, P approaches L.

108
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:23,591
Growth is fastest when P equals L over
two.

109
00:08:24,041 --> 00:08:26,644
That's the inflection point of the
S-shaped curve.

110
00:08:27,766 --> 00:08:33,712
A rumor spreads in a school of one
